ssed the entomologist Henry  Bates work on protective mimicry mimicry  in Amazonian butterflies one species of  butterfly toothsome two predatory birds  had markings very similar to another  much more populous species that was  noxious to those same birds the  historian Stefan Muller villa has noted  darwin's remark in his review of bates  first paper on this subject that the  similarity was so great that mr. Bates  says Darwin with his eyes sharpened by  collecting during 11 years was though  always on my guard  thus Bates continually deceived Bates in  other words on Darwin's view clearly  possessed taxonomic intuitions much like  those of Amazonian birds even when they  were misled now this commonality of  human classification compulsions  those animals shows why Darwin was so  comfortable with seconding the aesthetic  preferences of peahens anthropomorphism  of this kind was neither naive nor  capricious instead certain things were  simply what animals including humans  found beautiful but how could that kind  of unity justify taxonomic judgments of  similarity or difference how could it  give force to the compulsions of the  naturalist Darwin in effect naturalized  the so called natural system in the end  whatever naturalist tended to do was in  the main determined by the mysterious  compulsions trained by natural selection  but in practice this meant that Darwin  was happy to rest some confidence in the  general classification tendencies  expressed by people who were in fact  appropriately socialized members of the  European Natural History community the  natural system might not have reflected  God's plan in Darwin's view but it did  reflect in a durkheimian sort of way  perhaps the judgments of his own  scientific colleagues those colleagues  were Darwin's mirror of nature ok thank  you

